# ==============================================================================
# Terraform
# ==============================================================================

# Provider binaries and module cache. Large (hundreds of MB) and reproducible.
**/.terraform/*

# Local state. The examples run without a backend, so these would contain group
# object IDs, UPNs and access package policy in cleartext.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
.terraform.tfstate.lock.info

# Plan files. Same sensitive values as state.
*.tfplan
*.tfplan.*
plan.out

# Crash logs from Terraform and from providers.
crash.log
crash.*.log

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Variable files
#
# terraform.tfvars is ignored HERE, in a module repo, because this repo's own root is a
# development and split-state harness and its tfvars is scaffolding.
#
# In a CUSTOMER root the opposite applies: terraform.tfvars is committed, because for an
# access system the configuration is the governance record — the reviewable answer to who
# may reach what. It holds no secrets; object IDs and UPNs are not credentials.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!*.tfvars.example
!terraform.tfvars.example

# Backend configuration. Names the storage account holding both repos' state, so it
# is kept out even though it holds no secret by itself.
backend.hcl
!backend.hcl.example

# CLI config, which can contain credentials or mirror settings.
.terraformrc
terraform.rc

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lock files — NOT committed, anywhere, in any of the three repos
#
# Reversal of the earlier rule that the root lock file must be committed. There is
# now one source of truth for provider versions: required_providers. A lock file is
# a second one, and two sources of truth for the same fact drift.
#
# Note that the .terraform rule above does NOT cover this. ".terraform/" matches a
# directory; ".terraform.lock.hcl" is a sibling FILE, not something inside it. That
# is why lock files kept reappearing despite the directory being ignored, and why
# this needs its own line.
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS. Recorded here, at the decision, rather than in a PR description:
#
#   * No checksum verification of provider binaries. The zh:/h1: hashes in the lock
#     file were the only integrity check on the download. Nothing now detects a
#     tampered or substituted provider package.
#
#   * A past deploy cannot be reproduced exactly. Patch releases inside the
#     constraint will differ between runs, so "the version that was applied" is not
#     recoverable from the repo alone — only the range it fell within.
#
# The price of dropping the lock file is that constraints must stand on their own:
# ROOT configurations pin to PATCH level (three segments, ~> 3.9.0, not ~> 3.9),
# and every provider the configuration resolves must be declared in the root even
# if the root never references it. An undeclared provider is now unbounded forever,
# because there is no lock file left to pin it accidentally.
#
# Reusable modules still use >=, so they never become a ceiling for a consumer.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.terraform.lock.hcl

# ==============================================================================
# Credentials and environment
# ==============================================================================

.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
azure-credentials.json
sp-credentials.json

# ==============================================================================
# Tooling and OS
# ==============================================================================

# Intermediate files from diagram generation. PNG and .drawio are committed
# deliberately.
generated-diagrams/*.dot

.DS_Store
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
